cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code, dataset, or paper, please cite it as below."
title: "Plan-Then-Compile: Structured Reasoning Traces for FHIR-to-SQL Fine-Tuning"
type: software
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-30"
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/adelelsayed/fhirsql-reasoning-sql"
abstract: >-
  A fine-tuning study on translating natural-language hospital questions into
  DuckDB SQL against a FHIR-derived schema, building on a prior study
  (Elsayed 2026a, "From Frozen to Fluent") by retargeting SFT and DAPO/GRPO
  RL to produce a structured JSON query plan followed by the compiled SQL,
  in one continuous completion, giving RL a genuine reasoning trace to
  refine rather than a single flat SQL guess. Across three seeds, SFT lifts
  structural query-plan correctness from 0% (frozen base model) to 99.6% on
  familiar clinical concepts and 82.1% on concepts never seen in training,
  eliminates terminology-hardcoding entirely via an explicit reward penalty
  (0.0% vs. the frozen model's 3.0-3.4%), and reaches perfect abstention
  precision/recall (100%/100%). A subsequent DAPO/GRPO RL stage again shows
  no measurable gain over SFT -- the same finding as the prior study, now
  replicated under a design built specifically to give RL a reasoning trace
  to work with, strengthening rather than merely repeating the result. A
  database-verified error analysis attributes 78% of residual unseen-concept
  failures to a single benign surface-form pattern (over-applying a SNOMED
  CT qualifier-suffix convention to concepts coded in other terminology
  systems), not diffuse reasoning failure.
